From effef88556ba528bc294dc8ab72f10dc60ad7511 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mikhail Malyshev <mike.malyshev@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 11:34:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] vfio/igd: publish the host stolen base to firmware

The iGPU keeps firmware-programmed pointers into stolen memory - the GuC
WOPCM window and the RC6 context - that no BAR quirk can relocate, because
the hardware anchors them to its own physical stolen base rather than to
anything the guest driver programs. The driver writes only relative
offsets and sizes, which the hardware then adds to that fixed base.

The consequence is that guest stolen memory placed at an arbitrary address
leaves those accesses pointing at host physical addresses which, in the
guest's IOVA space, are ordinary guest RAM. The GPU then reads and writes
whatever the guest happens to keep there.

Publish the host stolen base as etc/igd-stolen-base so the firmware can
reserve the guest stolen region at exactly that address. With guest and
host stolen coincident, every value the hardware emits is already correct
in the guest's IOVA space, nothing needs relocating, and the existing
relocation quirks reduce to identity.

Firmware is free to ignore this and allocate anywhere, which restores the
previous behaviour.

Signed-off-by: Mikhail Malyshev <mike.malyshev@gmail.com>
---
 hw/vfio/igd.c | 33 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 33 insertions(+)

diff --git a/tools/qemu-xen/hw/vfio/igd.c b/tools/qemu-xen/hw/vfio/igd.c
index 8997611920..4e5b30ba4c 100644
--- a/tools/qemu-xen/hw/vfio/igd.c
+++ b/tools/qemu-xen/hw/vfio/igd.c
@@ -677,6 +677,39 @@ void vfio_probe_igd_bar4_quirk(VFIOPCIDevice *vdev, int nr)
                                 bdsm_size, sizeof(*bdsm_size));
                 error_report("IGD bdsm-size: wrote etc/igd-bdsm-size = %"
                              PRIu64 " bytes to fw_cfg", bdsm_bytes);
+
+                /*
+                 * Publish the HOST stolen base so the firmware can reserve the
+                 * guest stolen region at the very same physical address.
+                 *
+                 * The iGPU keeps firmware-programmed pointers into stolen
+                 * memory (WOPCM, the RC6 context) that no BAR quirk can move:
+                 * the hardware anchors them to its own physical stolen base.
+                 * If guest stolen lands anywhere else, those accesses hit
+                 * whatever the guest happens to have at the host address.
+                 *
+                 * Reserving guest stolen at the host address makes the guest's
+                 * IOVA space agree with what the hardware emits, so no value
+                 * needs relocating at all and the relocation quirks collapse to
+                 * identity.  Firmware falls back to allocating anywhere if it
+                 * cannot honour the address.
+                 */
+                if (gen >= 11) {
+                    uint64_t hb = 0;
+
+                    if (pread(vdev->vbasedev.fd, &hb, 8,
+                              vdev->config_offset + IGD_BDSM_GEN11) == 8) {
+                        hb = le64_to_cpu(hb) & ~0xfffULL;
+                    }
+                    if (hb) {
+                        uint64_t *base = g_malloc(sizeof(*base));
+                        *base = cpu_to_le64(hb);
+                        fw_cfg_add_file(fw_cfg, "etc/igd-stolen-base",
+                                        base, sizeof(*base));
+                        error_report("IGD stolen-base: wrote "
+                                     "etc/igd-stolen-base = 0x%" PRIx64, hb);
+                    }
+                }
             } else {
                 error_report("IGD bdsm-size: fw_cfg not found, skipping");
             }
-- 
